BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314149273@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260508T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260508T140000
SUMMARY:Garrison Creek\, Shaw Street and Little Portugal (NEW for 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nBuried bridges\, old Lake 
 Iroquois\, Humewood Reach\, Springmount Reach\, Taddle Creek\, stories fro
 m Toronto's pre-history. Travel back in time to a far different\, and natu
 ral Toronto. And then move forward to the fascinating enclave of Little Po
 rtugal and the history of the area prior to 1974! The history of this belo
 ved community is a story of heroism and heartache. This tour is an updated
  route of the separate Garrison Creek tour that was retired last year.\n\n
 Walk along a now hidden waterway and explore ravines in the heart of the c
 ity. Travel north on an adventure. Explore historic streets with a very di
 fferent perspective\, of what is beneath. An entire area of the city is bu
 ried\, and I bet you don't know where. Put it this way in the heart of the
  city you can find homes that have been buried for over 100 years!\n\nFoll
 ow the city streets we walk on every single day and understand the geologi
 c processes that have shaped Toronto. The rest as they say is history.\n\n
 This walking tour begins at the southeast **corner** of **Stanley Park** a
 t King Street West and Walnut Street\, north side\, west of Niagara Street
 . It ends at Christie Subway Station on Bloor Street West.\n\nThis walking
  tour is for all walking levels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to
  see you there.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\
 \*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your
  space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**t
 orontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/314149
 273/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260406T160219Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260406T160219Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_312801308@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260509T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260509T143000
SUMMARY:North Rosedale: Moore Park and the Brickworks!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the f
 ollowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nToronto's lungs
  are its ravines\, its green oasis\, breathing life into the City itself. 
 A city emerging from forest.\n\nFanning outward like the veins in a leaf\,
  Toronto's ravines bring a touch of the country to every city neighbourhoo
 d. They provide a tranquil sanctuary and respite from the noise and bustle
 \, and a safe home for birds\, and other animals.\n\nThe Moore Park or Bel
 t Line Ravine stretches from the Don Valley Parkway east of Rosedale to th
 e edge of Mount Pleasant Cemetery. It has faced destruction several times 
 in its long history. But cooler heads have prevailed. It served as an earl
 y commuter railway route between Toronto and its suburbs. But during the 1
 940s plans were underway to utilize the ravine for a major traffic artery 
 which was never built. For that we are truly thankful. A garden awaits us.
 \n\nTake a walk on the real wild side and let's follow and explore the old
  Moore Park Ravine and North Rosedale.\n\nThis walking tour is for all wal
 king levels and fitness skills\, but there are several hills on this walk.
  Please dress for the season and bring water. Hope to see you there.\n\nTh
 is walking tour begins outside the Castle Frank Subway Station on the Bloo
 r Danforth Line. We end this walk at the corner of Moore Park and Mount Pl
 easant Road\, adjacent to transit.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.
 00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket price
  when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the followin
 g email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/312801
 308/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260109T133013Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260109T133013Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313004927@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260510T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260510T140000
SUMMARY:Harbourfront: Our Grand Concourse! (MEGA-TOUR 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the f
 ollowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nSS Noronic (pic
 tured above)\, Little Norway Park\, Maple Leaf Baseball Stadium\, iconic n
 ames from the harbourfront\, and the list goes on! It was where Toronto go
 t started and ended in many ways\, both physically and emotionally. To thi
 s day we look at the waterfront as the place where Toronto begins. Its sou
 l! But the history of the waterfront has been troubled right from the firs
 t decade when Toronto was known as York and the dumping of garbage and was
 te into the Bay was common.\n\nToronto's Harbourfront has been the neighbo
 urhood that has changed the most over the last century. A place almost unr
 ecognizable forty years ago. The changes have been rapid and overwhelming.
 \n\nThe central harbour has been used for shipping and industrial purposes
  since York was founded in 1793. The original shoreline was just south of 
 Front Street. The area south to today's shoreline south of Queen's Quay wa
 s filled in with landfill\, creating new land and a host of new problems! 
 It has not been a happy co-existence.\n\nThe Pierre Elliot Trudeau governm
 ent announced the Harbourfront project\, similar to the Granville Island p
 roject in Vancouver\, BC. prior to 1972. The idea was to create a cultural
  and residential zone that would push the industrial companies and factori
 es out of the harbour lands. Well most of that did happen\, but was it all
  for the best? Let's explore!\n\nThis walking tour begins in Little Norway
  Park adjacent to Queen's Quay West\, at the foot of Bathurst Street\, and
  ends at Sugar Beach at the foot of Jarvis Street.\n\nThis walking tour is
  for all walking levels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to see you
  there.\n\nThis walk has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\\*\\* **
 Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket price when booking your space fo
 r this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohis
 torywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/313004
 927/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260122T152325Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260122T152325Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314558707@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T133000
SUMMARY:Mount Pleasant Cemetery (WEEKDAY)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nThe only autho
 rized cemeteries within York were Anglican or Roman Catholic. Citizens who
  did not belong to either church had no choice but to find burial arrangem
 ents outside York.\n\nThe General Burying Ground received Royal Assent in 
 1826 and land that came to be known as Potters Field was acquired and star
 ted operation soon afterwards. Over time\, additional cemetery lands were 
 added and in 1873\, a new cemetery available to all citizens was establish
 ed. The new cemetery was situated on the farm land of the Cawthra family n
 ear Yonge Street.\n\nThis walk is for all fitness levels. Please dress for
  the weather. This walk will begin and end outside the Yorkminster Park Ba
 ptist Church at 1585 Yonge Street just north of Heath Street.\n\nThis tour
  has a ticket price of $15.00 per person! This tour has space for **11** e
 xplorers\, so if you do plan on attending please secure your tickets early
 .\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when bookin
 g your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:*
 *\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/314558
 707/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260430T123824Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260430T123824Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_312804382@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260516T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260516T150000
SUMMARY:Noronic Disaster! Was it Arson? Interactive Game and Tour (77th AN
 NIVERSARY)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Explo
 rer Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tour
 s\, email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or 
 for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the tic
 ket amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to th
 e following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**This speci
 al anniversary tour commemorates the burning of the Noronic in Toronto har
 bour on the evening of September 16-17\, 1949! Seventy Seven years ago**\n
 \nFirst it was **Ambrose Small** and my team of brilliant detectives who t
 ried to figure it out. The walkers on this tour just about cracked the cas
 e. Well done my detectives.\n\nAnd then the case that shocked Canada and t
 he world\, the murder of **Burt Massey!** That was a tough assignment with
  15 characters all wanting their time in the sun! But now a new assignment
  for my brilliant detectives. The Harley Karulis Detective Agency needs yo
 ur help once again! Here is the folder with all the information\, down bel
 ow! Your assignment awaits! Keep reading.......\n\nSaturday September 17\,
  1949 in the early hours. A small harmless fire in a closet on C Deck erup
 ted into an inferno that destroyed the Canada Steamship Lines\, Noronic. I
 t was Toronto's greatest disaster.\n\nOn this tour we will meet and hear t
 he stories of passengers\, crew and firefighters who responded on that fat
 eful night. Our goal is to try and figure out the true story of what happe
 ned on C Deck in that same closet. Was it an accident or was it done on pu
 rpose? If it was indeed arson\, then why?\n\nThe Noronic was built by the 
 Northern Navigation Company\, a subsidiary of the Richelieu and Ontario Na
 vigation Company. It plied the waters of the Great Lakes since its first t
 rip in 1913. It carried millions of happy holiday makers\, but things were
  coming to an end. Could this be the motivation to end the service. Points
  to ponder!\n\nThe car was King or Queen\, and the second world war was ov
 er and attitudes were "a changing\," quickly! The days of the passenger cr
 uise ships on the Great Lakes were seen as part of the old world.\n\nAre y
 ou up for this interactive game once again?\n\nThis walking tour/game take
 s participants back in time to 1949 Toronto\, and explores the many sites 
 associated with this disaster and the personalities involved. You will be 
 introduced to 15 historic characters and each has their own dark secrets\,
  it will be up to you to uncover the truth.\n\n**This tour DOES not requir
 e a PRESTO card! All the sites are centred around the southern section of 
 the downtown core and harbour front!**\n\nOur walk begins outside the **We
 stin Harbour Castle Hotel** on Queen's Quay and Bay Street just east of th
 e Jack Layton Ferry Terminal on Queen's Quay East at the front doors of th
 e hotel. A video on the disaster will follow in the comments to introduce 
 you to this fascinating piece of Toronto history!\n\nThis tour has a ticke
 t price of $15.00 per person. Please get your tickets early if you would l
 ike to attend as this tour has a limit of 25 participants!\n\n\\*\\* **Ple
 ase note: Please e-transfer the ticket price when booking your space for t
 his tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistor
 ywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/312804
 382/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260109T172941Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260109T172941Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313367680@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260517T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260517T130000
SUMMARY:The Happy Tour! Yes the Happy Tour is Back
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the f
 ollowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nSo in the winte
 r of 2022 I listened to my guests. Time after time\, the question that cam
 e up was\, is there anything good and happy about history? Well not much\,
  it's always a bit on the horrific side\, and that is about all I can say\
 , for now!\n\nThings are a changing for the better. Let's make the rest of
  2026 a happy year! And with everything that has transpired in the last ye
 ar and a half with tariffs and other undesirables let's have a good old ti
 me. I have been told that I am wordy\, but that's ok.\n\nA tour about good
  times\, good times in the sun\, that's a bit of a stretch\, but I did it.
  I put together a fun tour of the sunny side of the street. A tour of the 
 good things in Toronto life from the Santa Claus Parade to decorated windo
 ws of both Eaton's and Simpson's\, to trips over to the Island\, and picni
 cs with little puppies and kittens in High Park. Ok that's going a bit far
 \, cut the puppies and the kittens!\n\nNo dark secrets\, no fires\, cut of
 f fingers\, floods\, tooth marks\, disasters\, murders\, horrific deaths o
 r innocent victims falling from windows\, or crushed under subway trains\,
  ghosts peeking out of buildings\, human vampires\, Spanish Flu or Spanish
  Civil War's impact on Toronto\, or graves\, I'm already starting to get h
 omesick for the good times with all this fun stuff.\n\nCome along and expe
 rience a different and fun Toronto. In this strange era we are living in\,
  a bit of sunshine is just the ticket. But no winter blues on this tour\, 
 I promise! You'll be as happy and contented as a **bunny in the sun.**\n\n
 And there is plenty of interesting history here in Toronto that is on the 
 positive and fun\, happy side\, you'll see.\n\nThis walking tour is for al
 l fitness levels and skills. Please dress for the weather. This walking to
 ur begins and ends on the steps of old City Hall at the northeast corner o
 f Queen Street West and Bay Street. Hope to see you soon!\n\nThis tour has
  a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-tr
 ansfer the ticket price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-t
 ransfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/313367
 680/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260215T135921Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260215T135921Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313005081@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260517T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260517T160000
SUMMARY:Historic Deer Park (RETURN FOR 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nIn 1837\, the 
 Heath family purchased 40 acres on the northwest corner of Yonge and St. C
 lair and named it Deer Park. The neighbourhood included a racetrack\, a sc
 hool\, and a hotel. Hotel guests could feed deer that roamed the land. The
  property was subdivided in 1846 and was sold off by 1874.\n\nIn 1891\, Up
 per Canada College moved from its downtown Toronto location to Deer Park\,
  and in 1931\, De La Salle College followed to settle on the estate 'Oakla
 nds' that was purchased and built by Senator John Macdonald. Oaklands form
 s part of the southern boundary of Deer Park.\n\nThis walk is for all fitn
 ess levels. The meeting place for our walking adventure is just outside th
 e Deer Park Library at 40 St. Clair Avenue East. We will return to the sta
 rting point after the walk which is adjacent to the TTC St. Clair Station.
 \n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please 
 note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this
  tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywa
 lks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/313005
 081/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260122T152730Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260122T152730Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313251527@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260523T150000
SUMMARY:The Lakeshore Psychiatric Hospital and Camp M (Special Extended To
 ur)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nElizabeth Simc
 oe named the area now known as Mimico for the thousands of pigeons that bl
 ackened the sky at the western end of the settlement. Mimico was originall
 y located at the confluence of Mimico Creek and Dundas Street.\n\nWilliam 
 Gamble\, opened a sawmill and built a settlement for the workers nearby. E
 tobicoke's first church\, Christ Church was opened on Church Street which 
 became Royal York Road. Mimico is a walkable neighbourhood and a great pla
 ce to stroll and discover our amazing history.\n\nThe Lakeshore Hospital w
 as built in 1888 as the Mimico Asylum\, its doors opened on January 21\, 1
 889.\n\nThe original idea for the hospital was a series of cottages design
 ed by Doctor Joseph Workman who wanted a resort type feel to the hospital.
  The architect was Kivas Tully\, who worked with gardener Samuel Matheson.
  Most of the buildings were built by the patients themselves\, and helped 
 with laundry duties\, and tended to the vast gardens.\n\nBut a different s
 tory emerged that most Canadians had no idea about. And that was the story
  of Camp M - Mimico.\n\nIn 1887\, the Victoria Industrial School for Boys 
 opened on old Horner Avenue in Mimico\, just blocks from the site of the n
 ew Mimico Hospital for the Insane (Humber College.) The school\, which was
  a juvenile reformatory\, hoped to break the bad boys through moral and ac
 ademic education and old fashioned hard work. I hope there was a positive 
 outcome.\n\nIn 1927\, the school changed its name to the Mimico Reform Sch
 ool. But it was the war years 1939-1945 that really surprised me as a Toro
 ntonian. It was known as Camp M or Internment Camp 22\, the largest camp f
 or POWs in the centre of a large Canadian city.\n\nMost camps were constru
 cted in rural areas\, and the camp provided space for more than 520 German
  nationals. The history of this camp is fascinating and had links to the T
 oronto Central Prison on Strachan Avenue and the Mimico Hospital directly 
 to the south on lakeshore where we begin this tour.\n\nThis walking tour i
 s for all levels and walkers. The tour begins at the New Toronto Public Li
 brary Branch at 110 Eleventh Street just south of Lakeshore. We end this t
 our at Kipling and Horner Avenue\, just a few blocks to the north of the o
 ld hospital!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\
 \* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your sp
 ace for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**toro
 ntohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/313251
 527/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260207T135059Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260207T135059Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313367429@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260524T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260524T140000
SUMMARY:Historic Leaside!
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the f
 ollowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**This tour has
  been revised and updated to reflect new scholarship on the history of the
  area.**\n\nJohn Lea Sr. was born in Lancashire England\, and immigrated t
 o York\, in 1819\, via Philadelphia. Mary Hutchison\, his wife\, and John 
 had their first child on May 28\, 1814 and named him William.\n\nLot 13 in
  the third concession was purchased from Alexander McDonnell on January 23
 \, 1820 and the new family\, John Lea Sr\, William\, John Jr. and Mary set
 tled the land. William Lea bought land to the south and began constructing
  a large\, octagonal brick house\, which he named Leaside. The house was c
 ompleted in 1854. John Lea Sr. died soon after.\n\nThe property became the
  possession of William's eldest son Joseph in 1893\, and he lived in the h
 ouse until 1903. In 1913 it was abandoned and demolished by Canadian North
 ern Railway. The company set fire to the historic landmark in order to cle
 ar the land for the townsite. A shame indeed! It seems that we have a **hi
 story** of eliminating our history piece by piece!\n\nLeaside was also\, i
 n addition to Liberty Village to the west\, part of the great armament ind
 ustry that contributed to the victory in world War 2.\n\nLeaside is one of
  Toronto's most historic neighbourhoods\, with plenty to discover and expl
 ore. Make the most of winter and let's walk and learn about an area of Tor
 onto that is not well known\, and certainly not on the history walking tou
 rs agenda for most companies.\n\nThis walking tour is for all levels and w
 alkers. The tour begins and ends at the Leaside Branch of the Toronto Publ
 ic Library at 165 McRae Drive\, north of Millwood Road. We will return to 
 this location after our walk!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 pe
 r person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket price when
  booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following ema
 il at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/313367
 429/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260215T134440Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260215T134440Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_313007915@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260505T032509Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260530T153000
SUMMARY:Toronto History with a Twist: Historical Improv! (RETURN FOR 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\n**Let's begin 
 the summer season with this creative walk that truly connects us all.** Th
 is walk allows walkers to jump into Toronto history itself and become the 
 players that made Toronto. The last time I did this tour\, I got so many p
 ositive remarks. Walkers had the time of their lives and learned so much m
 ore about Toronto! So let's do this one again!\n\nCalling all aspiring act
 ors and actresses\, Hollywood bound dreamers\, anyone with a passion to ac
 t. Have you caught the acting bug? If so and this sounds like you then ste
 p up to the acting plate. And you don't even have had to act\, just want s
 omething new to help connect us all.\n\nWhy not walk\, get your steps in\,
  and see Toronto from a totally different and fun perspective. Becoming th
 e history characters we all know and love.\n\n**Historical Improv.** This 
 is a different walking tour. Sounds good\, sounds way different\, right? T
 he best way to understand history is to become history.\n\nWhy not exercis
 e the imagination with a few 2 or 3-minute sketches and improv situations 
 along the route. Let's meet Mr. Simcoe\, his wife Elizabeth\, the Queen's 
 Rangers as they create a path through the wilderness. These improv sketche
 s are based on historical facts. They will help convert the shy\, build up
  the confidence\, create laughter and merriment\, (merriment might be a bi
 t strong) call it street theatre. What more is there?\n\nYou never know wh
 en the casting agent\, or Hollywood is **watching** peeking around the cor
 ner waiting for **you.**\n\nThis walk is for all walking levels. Please dr
 ess for the weather\, and hope to see you there.\n\nThis fun and unique wa
 lking tour begins and ends at Little Norway Park near the foot of Bathurst
  Street\, and Queen's Quay West.\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00
